. Source: Mother Jones mass-shootings dataset (1982-2026); row sources: https://www.nytimes.com/2025/12/19/us/mit-professor-shooting-suspect-portugal-lisbon-physics.html; https://www.nytimes.c.... Coordinates city-level (geocoded from the dataset place name).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#X">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2025-12-13</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.41283,41.82399,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Austin bar shooting - Austin, Texas</name><styleUrl>#t-mass-shooting</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Mass shootings</b> &middot; mass-shooting</p><p>3/1/2026. Venue type: other. Killed: 3. Injured: 13. Total victims: 16. Ndiaga Diagne, 53, opened fire in a popular entertainment district along Sixth Street, and was quickly killed in a gun battle with police. Diagne had emigrated to the US in 2000 and became a naturalized citizen in 2013. Source: Mother Jones mass-shootings dataset (1982-2026); row sources: https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/02/us/austin-mass-shooting-ndiaga-diagne-wwk-hnk. Coordinates city-level (geocoded from the dataset place name).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#X">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2026-03-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-97.7437,30.27113,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>San Diego mosque shooting - San Diego, California</name><styleUrl>#t-mass-shooting</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Mass shootings</b> &middot; mass-shooting</p><p>5/18/2026. Venue type: religious. Killed: 3. Injured: 0. Total victims: 3. Cain Clark, 17, and Caleb Vasquez, 18, attacked a mosque and then died by self-inflicted gunshots as police responded. Source: Mother Jones mass-shootings dataset (1982-2026); row sources: https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2026-05-18/san-diego-islamic-center-shooting-suspects-what-we-know; https://www.... Coordinates city-level (geocoded from the dataset place name).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#X">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2026-05-18</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-117.1655,32.8205,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>In-N-Out Burger shooting - Twin Falls, Idaho</name><styleUrl>#t-mass-shooting</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Mass shootings</b> &middot; mass-shooting</p><p>8/1/2026. Venue type: other. Killed: 3. Injured: 7. Total victims: 10. Chad Williams, 24, opened fire outside the restaurant, faced return fire from an armed citizen and an off-duty police officer, and shot himself to death. Source: Mother Jones mass-shootings dataset (1982-2026); row sources: https://abcnews.com/US/burger-shooting-suspect/story?id=135337720; https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/02/us/gunman-acted-al.... Coordinates city-level (geocoded from the dataset place name).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#X">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2026-08-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-114.4555,42.5971,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark></Folder><Folder><name>Sport and gambling (73)</name><open>0</open><Placemark><name>Flamingo Hotel - Bugsy Siegel / Meyer Lansky</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Casino opened Dec 26, 1946 (hotel March 1947); the third resort on the Strip and the oldest still operating. Bugsy Siegel took over the project from Billy Wilkerson, with Meyer Lansky&#x27;s approval as an off-the-books financier. Siegel was shot dead by an unknown gunman at a Beverly Hills home on June 20, 1947; the killing is unsolved.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1946-12-26</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-115.17056,36.11611,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Stardust Casino - Frank Rosenthal / Chicago Outfit</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Argent Corporation (Allen Glick) bought the Stardust in 1974 with Teamsters pension-fund loans; executive Frank &#x27;Lefty&#x27; Rosenthal ran casino operations at the Stardust and other Argent properties. A skimming operation was uncovered from 1976; members of organized crime were later convicted over the Las Vegas skim, but Rosenthal was never charged in connection with it. Rosenthal survived a car bomb on Oct 4, 1982. Depicted in &#x27;Casino&#x27; (1995). The Stardust closed Nov 1, 2006; its towers were imploded March 13, 2007.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1982-10-04</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-115.1631,36.1367,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Caesars Palace - Jay Sarno / Teamsters Loans</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Opened Aug 5, 1966. Jay Sarno and Stanley Mallin built it with a $10.6M loan (1962) from the Teamsters Central States Pension Fund. Allen Dorfman, the fund&#x27;s insurance administrator and a close associate of Jimmy Hoffa, was convicted in Dec 1982 of conspiring to bribe U.S. Senator Howard Cannon and was shot dead in a Lincolnwood, Illinois hotel parking lot on Jan 20, 1983, three days before sentencing; the killing is unsolved. Hoffa disappeared in 1975.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1966-08-05</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-115.1745,36.1162,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Havana Casinos - Lansky/Trafficante Pre-Revolution</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Meyer Lansky was associated with the casinos at the Hotel Nacional and the Hotel Riviera in Havana; Santo Trafficante Jr. with the Sans Souci and others. The revolution (Jan 1, 1959) closed the mob-linked casino trade. Where that capital and expertise went after 1959 is a question, not a finding.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1959-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-82.3666,23.1136,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>T-Mobile Arena / Las Vegas Strip - Sports Capital</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Opened Apr 6, 2016 - home of the Vegas Golden Knights (NHL, from 2017). Las Vegas now also hosts the Raiders (NFL, 2020, Allegiant Stadium), the Aces (WNBA), NBA Summer League, the F1 Grand Prix, Super Bowl LVIII (2024), and the planned relocation of the Athletics (MLB). The leagues that long kept away from Las Vegas now hold sports-betting partnerships.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2016-04-06</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-115.1784,36.1029,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Allegiant Stadium - Las Vegas Raiders</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>$1.9 billion stadium; $750M of public money raised through a Clark County hotel-room tax (SB1, signed Oct 17, 2016). The Raiders moved from Oakland in 2020; owner Mark Davis, son of Al Davis. Sheldon Adelson (d. 2021) of Las Vegas Sands first backed the project and pledged $650M of his own money, then pulled out in early 2017. The NFL, which suspended players for gambling, relocated a franchise to the gambling capital, with the public paying the largest single share of the stadium.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2020-01-01</begin><end>2020-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-115.1833,36.0909,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>NBA HQ - 645 Fifth Ave, New York</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>National Basketball Association headquarters (also Secaucus, NJ). Commissioner Adam Silver (since Feb 2014) wrote a 2014 New York Times op-ed advocating legal, regulated sports betting. Referee Tim Donaghy pleaded guilty in 2007 to federal charges for betting on games and passing information to gamblers; reports of mob involvement were later discounted. The league now has official betting partnerships with sportsbooks. Silver&#x27;s position: regulate what happens anyway.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2014-01-01</begin><end>2014-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.9747,40.761,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Tim Donaghy - West Chester, Pennsylvania</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>FBI investigation (2007): NBA referee Tim Donaghy bet on games he officiated (2003-07) and passed information to gamblers James Battista and Thomas Martino, high-school classmates. Pleaded guilty Aug 15, 2007; sentenced to 15 months (July 29, 2008). Early reports of mob ties were later discounted; Donaghy alleged other referees and the league office influenced outcomes - the NBA denied it. Question, not finding: one referee went undetected for years.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2007-01-01</begin><end>2007-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-75.6057,39.9607,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Boston College - 1978-79 Point Shaving</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>1978-79 season: Pittsburgh gamblers Rocco and Tony Perla recruited Boston College player Rick Kuhn; Paul Mazzei brought in Henry Hill and Jimmy Burke of the Lucchese crime family. Gamblers bet against BC on fixed games - the team often still won, just not by the spread. Convicted in 1981: Burke (12 years), Kuhn (10 years, reduced), Mazzei and Tony Perla (10 years), Rocco Perla (4 years); Hill testified. Retold in &#x27;Goodfellas&#x27;.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1978-01-01</begin><end>1978-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-71.1685,42.3355,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Tulane University - 1985 Point Shaving</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Feb 1985: Tulane players shaved points in two home games (Southern Miss Feb 6, Memphis State Feb 20) after student Gary Kranz, who had supplied some of them with cocaine, offered cash; several thousand dollars changed hands. Kranz and others pleaded guilty; star John &#x27;Hot Rod&#x27; Williams was acquitted at trial. President Eamon Kelly disbanded the program on April 4, 1985; it returned in 1989.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1985-02-01</begin><end>1985-02-28</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-90.1208,29.9401,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Arizona State - 1994 Point Shaving</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Former ASU student and campus bookmaker Benny Silman paid guards Stevin &#x27;Hedake&#x27; Smith and Isaac Burton to shave points in four games in the 1993-94 season; the team won but failed to cover. Chicago-area bettors were also convicted; Silman says organized-crime figures pressured him to continue. Silman was sentenced to 46 months; Smith to a year and a day.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1994-01-01</begin><end>1994-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-111.9281,33.4242,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>NFL HQ - 345 Park Ave, New York</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>National Football League headquarters. Commissioner Roger Goodell repeatedly opposed sports betting until the revenue opportunity became clear. The NFL suspended players for gambling (Paul Hornung 1963, Alex Karras 1963, Art Schlichter, Josh Shaw, Calvin Ridley, etc.) while simultaneously accepting $1B+ annually in gambling advertising revenue. FanDuel, DraftKings, Caesars are all &#x27;official partners.&#x27; The league that suspends players for betting profits from the betting industry. $20.2B league revenue (2023).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1963-01-01</begin><end>1963-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.9725,40.7578,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>1958 NFL Championship - &#x27;Greatest Game Ever Played&#x27;</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Yankee Stadium, Dec 28, 1958. Baltimore Colts 23, New York Giants 17 (OT) - the game that made the NFL a television product. Colts owner Carroll Rosenbloom was a known gambler; it has been alleged, never established, that he bet on the game. He drowned off Golden Beach, Florida on April 2, 1979 - ruled an accident; a PBS Frontline documentary later suggested he may have been murdered.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1958-12-28</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9262,40.8296,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Art Schlichter - Gambling / Organized Crime</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Ohio State quarterback, 4th overall pick (1982, Baltimore Colts). Lost hundreds of thousands betting; went to the FBI about his bookies in 1983 and was suspended by the NFL (1983). Arrested in New York in 1987 over a sports-betting operation (probation). In 2011 pleaded guilty to state theft/corrupt-activity charges (10 years) and to federal wire-fraud charges over a ticket scheme; federal sentence 10 years 7 months (May 2012), concurrent.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1982-01-01</begin><end>1982-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-82.9988,39.9612,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Calvin Ridley - NFL Gambling Suspension</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Atlanta Falcons WR suspended indefinitely, through at least the 2022 season, for betting on NFL games in 2021, including on his own team (reported as ~$1,500 in parlays); not accused of fixing anything. Forfeited his $11.1M 2022 salary. The league that suspended him holds sportsbook partnerships.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2022-01-01</begin><end>2022-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-84.388,33.749,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Jerry Jones - Dallas Cowboys / Comstock Resources</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>AT&amp;T Stadium, Arlington TX. Jones bought the Cowboys for $140M (Feb 1989); Forbes has since valued them as the world&#x27;s most valuable sports team (~$10.1B). Jones is also the controlling shareholder of Comstock Resources (oil/gas) through entities he solely owns (~70.9%, 2026 proxy). Pattern: NFL ownership as one holding among energy, real estate, finance and media wealth.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1989-01-01</begin><end>1989-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-97.0945,32.7473,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Robert Kraft - New England Patriots / Kraft Group</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Gillette Stadium, Foxborough MA. Kraft bought the Patriots for $172M (1994). Kraft Group: paper, packaging, real estate, private equity. Feb 2019: charged with two misdemeanor counts of soliciting prostitution at Orchids of Asia Day Spa, Jupiter FL; police had entered the spa under the banner of a human-trafficking investigation, but prosecutors said in April 2019 there was no evidence of trafficking. Charges dropped in 2020 after Florida courts suppressed the hidden-camera video.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2019-02-01</begin><end>2019-02-28</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-71.2643,42.0909,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Stan Kroenke - LA Rams / Arsenal / KSE</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>SoFi Stadium, Inglewood CA. Kroenke owns the LA Rams, Denver Nuggets, Colorado Avalanche, Colorado Rapids, Colorado Mammoth and Arsenal FC; KSE agreed in Sept 2026 to buy the Los Angeles Angels (reported; close expected 2027, pending MLB approval). Married to Ann Walton Kroenke, a Walmart heiress. Cross-border sports ownership as financial network mapping.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-118.339,33.9535,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Comiskey Park - 1919 Black Sox Scandal</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>1919 World Series: eight Chicago White Sox players were accused of losing to the Cincinnati Reds for gamblers&#x27; money. Arnold Rothstein&#x27;s involvement is contested - there is evidence both for and against it. The players were acquitted in court (1921) but banned for life by Commissioner Landis. Rothstein was shot at the Park Central Hotel, NYC, on Nov 4, 1928.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1919-10-01</begin><end>1919-10-28</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-87.6338,41.8299,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Arnold Rothstein - Park Central Hotel, NYC</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Arnold Rothstein, widely reputed to have organized the fix of the 1919 World Series, was shot at the Park Central Hotel on Nov 4, 1928 and died Nov 6. A mentor to Lucky Luciano, Meyer Lansky, Frank Costello and Bugsy Siegel, he is credited with turning organized crime into a business run like a corporation.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1928-11-04</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9794,40.7648,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Pete Rose - Gambling Ban / Hit King</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Riverfront Stadium / Great American Ball Park, Cincinnati. Rose - MLB&#x27;s all-time hits leader (4,256) - was banned for life by Commissioner Giamatti on Aug 24, 1989 for betting on baseball, including games he managed. He denied it until 2004. Giamatti died of a heart attack on Sept 1, 1989. Rose died Sept 30, 2024; on May 13, 2025 MLB ended permanent bans at death, reinstating him posthumously. MLB now has official partnerships with legal sportsbooks.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1989-08-24</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-84.5069,39.0976,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>BALCO Labs - Burlingame, California</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Bay Area Laboratory Co-Operative. Founder Victor Conte distributed the designer steroid THG (&#x27;the Clear&#x27;) and other drugs to athletes; the 2003 federal investigation reached MLB, NFL and Olympic sports, and several athletes, including Marion Jones, later admitted use. Barry Bonds testified to the 2003 grand jury and denied knowingly using steroids; his obstruction conviction was overturned.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2003-01-01</begin><end>2003-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-122.3661,37.5841,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>MLB HQ - 1271 Ave of the Americas, NYC</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Major League Baseball headquarters. MLB has official sports-betting partnerships with several sportsbooks (incl. DraftKings, FanDuel, BetMGM). Commissioner Rob Manfred. The league that banned Pete Rose for life for betting now earns partnership revenue from legal betting.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-73.979,40.7614,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Houston Astros - Sign Stealing Scandal</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Daikin Park (formerly Minute Maid Park), Houston. MLB found (Jan 13, 2020) the Astros used a camera system to steal signs in 2017 and part of 2018, relaying them by banging a trash can. They won the 2017 World Series. Manager AJ Hinch and GM Jeff Luhnow were suspended one year and fired; players received immunity for cooperating. The title stands.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2017-01-01</begin><end>2017-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-95.3555,29.7572,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>NCAA HQ - Indianapolis, Indiana</name><styleUrl>#t-governance</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; governance</p><p>National Collegiate Athletic Association headquarters. Walter Byers, the NCAA&#x27;s first executive director, wrote that the NCAA developed the term &#x27;student-athlete&#x27; to insulate colleges from injured players&#x27; disability claims. NCAA v. Alston (2021, unanimous) held that NCAA limits on education-related benefits violate antitrust law; Justice Kavanaugh&#x27;s concurrence said the NCAA&#x27;s business model would be flatly illegal in almost any other industry.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2023-01-01</begin><end>2023-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-86.149,39.8105,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>CCNY - 1950-51 Point Shaving Scandal</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>City College of New York. The 1950-51 investigation exposed point shaving at at least seven schools: CCNY, LIU, NYU, Manhattan, Kentucky, Bradley, Toledo. Gamblers included Salvatore Sollazzo and Eli &#x27;Kaye&#x27; Klukofsky. CCNY had won both the NIT and NCAA tournament in 1950 - the only team ever to do so. Kentucky&#x27;s program was suspended for the 1952-53 season.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1950-01-01</begin><end>1950-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.95,40.8198,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>University of Kentucky - Rupp Arena</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>UK basketball — the most storied program in college basketball history — was implicated in the 1951 point-shaving scandal. Coach Adolph Rupp initially claimed the fixers &#x27;couldn&#x27;t touch my boys with a ten-foot pole.&#x27; Three players were arrested. The NCAA suspended the program for the entire 1952-53 season. Judge Saul Streit called UK&#x27;s program &#x27;the acme of commercialism and overemphasis&#x27; — in 1951. 75 years later, UK basketball generates $50M+ in annual revenue. The commercialism the judge identified has been formalized, not reformed.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1951-01-01</begin><end>1951-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-84.50278,38.04944,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Northwestern University - 1995 Point Shaving</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Evanston, IL. In 1995 former Notre Dame kicker Kevin Pendergast, deep in gambling debt, persuaded Northwestern players Kenneth Dion Lee and Dewey Williams to shave points; Pendergast pleaded guilty (1998) to conspiring to fix three games.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1995-01-01</begin><end>1995-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-87.6753,42.0565,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>University of San Diego - Gambling Ring (2011)</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>April 2011 federal indictment: former USD assistant coach Thaddeus &#x27;T.J.&#x27; Brown (on staff 2006-07), former star Brandon Johnson and others were charged with sports bribery over games in the 2009-10 season. Johnson was sentenced to six months. Point shaving did not stop after each decade&#x27;s scandal.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2011-01-01</begin><end>2011-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-117.188,32.772,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>FIFA HQ - Zurich, Switzerland</name><styleUrl>#t-governance</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; governance</p><p>Federation Internationale de Football Association. May 27, 2015: the DOJ indicted 14 FIFA officials and associates on 47 counts including racketeering, wire fraud and money laundering; Swiss police arrested seven officials at the Baur au Lac hotel. The indictment described corruption &#x27;rampant, systemic, and deep-rooted&#x27; spanning 24 years, prosecuted under RICO - the statute written for the Mafia. FIFA is a private association under Swiss law.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2015-05-27</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.5749,47.3815,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Baur au Lac Hotel - FIFA Arrests, Zurich</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>May 27, 2015: Swiss police arrested seven FIFA officials at dawn on U.S. corruption charges as they gathered for the 65th FIFA Congress and presidential election; the U.S. alleged more than $150M in bribes. Hotel staff reportedly held up sheets to shield them from cameras.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2015-05-27</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.54,47.3658,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>CONMEBOL HQ - Luque, Paraguay</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>South American football confederation. Three successive presidents were indicted in the 2015 U.S. case: Nicolas Leoz (charged 2015, died 2019 in Paraguay without being extradited), Eugenio Figueredo, and Juan Angel Napout (convicted Dec 2017 of racketeering and wire-fraud conspiracy, sentenced 2018 to 9 years). Copa America and Copa Libertadores media rights were sold through bribe-mediated deals.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2015-01-01</begin><end>2015-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-57.5161,-25.2606,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>CONCACAF HQ - Miami, Florida</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>North/Central American football confederation. Jack Warner (president 1990-2011, Trinidad) and Chuck Blazer (general secretary 1990-2011, USA). Warner was indicted in 2015 and has been fighting extradition from Trinidad; a U.S. court ordered him to repay CONCACAF US$79M in a 2019 default judgment. Blazer became an FBI cooperating witness, pleaded guilty to racketeering, wire fraud, tax evasion and money laundering, and died in 2017 before sentencing. Its top two officials for 21 years: one indicted and abroad, the other a federal informant.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1990-01-01</begin><end>1990-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-80.138,25.773,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Trump Tower - Chuck Blazer&#x27;s Apartment</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Fifth Avenue, NYC. Chuck Blazer (CONCACAF general secretary, FIFA executive committee) kept two apartments on the 49th floor - one reportedly for his cats. He failed to file U.S. tax returns for years, became an FBI cooperating witness (from 2011) and pleaded guilty to tax evasion, racketeering, wire fraud and money laundering; he died in 2017.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2011-01-01</begin><end>2011-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.9742,40.7625,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Qatar - 2022 FIFA World Cup</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>FIFA awarded Qatar the 2022 World Cup on Dec 2, 2010. A 2020 U.S. indictment alleged bribes to FIFA executive-committee members to vote for Qatar. The Guardian (Feb 2021) found 6,500+ migrant workers from India, Pakistan, Nepal, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka died in Qatar since 2010 - across all sectors, during a building programme largely for the tournament; 37 deaths were directly linked to stadium construction. 69% of Indian, Nepali and Bangladeshi deaths were recorded as &#x27;natural&#x27;. Workers were bound by the kafala sponsorship system.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2010-12-02</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>51.1839,25.3548,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Russia - 2018 FIFA World Cup</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>FIFA awarded Russia the 2018 World Cup on Dec 2, 2010, in the same vote as Qatar. A 2020 U.S. indictment alleged Jack Warner received $5M in wire transfers to vote for Russia. FIFA&#x27;s own inquiry, the Garcia Report (2014), was kept secret and summarized in a way Garcia called &#x27;materially incomplete&#x27; with &#x27;erroneous representations&#x27;. Russia hosted the tournament four years after annexing Crimea, with the Donbas war ongoing.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2010-12-02</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.6173,55.7558,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Bochum, Germany - Match Fixing Hub</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Nov 2009: Bochum prosecutors and German police exposed a match-fixing network covering about 200 fixtures in nine European countries, including UEFA Europa League and Champions League qualifiers - &#x27;the biggest match-fixing scandal ever to hit Europe&#x27; (UEFA). Croatian brothers Ante and Milan Sapina were arrested on Nov 19, 2009; Ante Sapina was sentenced to five and a half years (May 2011). Bets were placed with Asian bookmakers.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2009-01-01</begin><end>2009-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>7.2162,51.4818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Singapore - Dan Tan / Global Match Fixing</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Dan Tan Seet Eng - charged in Italy (2011) and Hungary over match fixing; Interpol called him the &#x27;boss&#x27; of the world&#x27;s largest and most aggressive match-fixing syndicate. Singapore, which has no extradition treaty with the EU, detained him without trial in 2013; its Court of Appeal ordered his release in Nov 2015.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2013-01-01</begin><end>2013-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>103.8198,1.3521,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Calciopoli - Rome / Italian Football Fixing</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>2006 Italian football scandal: wiretaps showed Juventus general manager Luciano Moggi working FIGC and referee-designator contacts to influence referee selection. Juventus was stripped of two Serie A titles and relegated; AC Milan, Fiorentina, Lazio, Reggina and Arezzo were penalized. Moggi was banned by sporting courts; his criminal case ended in 2015 with the main charge time-barred.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2006-01-01</begin><end>2006-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>IOC HQ - Lausanne, Switzerland</name><styleUrl>#t-governance</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; governance</p><p>International Olympic Committee headquarters. A private association under Swiss law with special recognized status - the same Swiss-seat pattern as FIFA and the BIS. IOC members are a self-electing body. The IOC chooses host cities and its &#x27;official partners&#x27;.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2017-01-01</begin><end>2017-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>6.6323,46.5197,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Salt Lake City - 2002 Olympic Bribery Scandal</name><styleUrl>#t-organized-crime</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; organized-crime</p><p>Late 1998: it emerged that Salt Lake&#x27;s bid committee had given IOC members and relatives cash, scholarships and gifts to win the 2002 Winter Games. The IOC recommended expelling six members. Bid leaders Tom Welch and Dave Johnson were charged with 15 counts of bribery and fraud and acquitted of all charges in December 2003.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1998-01-01</begin><end>1998-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-111.891,40.7608,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Rio de Janeiro - 2016 Olympics / Corruption</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>2016 Summer Olympics. Brazilian authorities say Brazilian Olympic Committee president Carlos Nuzman was central to channelling $2M to IOC member Lamine Diack to buy votes for Rio&#x27;s 2009 selection. Arrested Oct 2017 (released to house arrest after 15 days); sentenced Nov 2021 to 30 years 11 months for corruption, organized crime, money laundering and currency evasion.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2016-01-01</begin><end>2016-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-43.1729,-22.9068,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Sochi - 2014 Winter Olympics / $51 Billion</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Most expensive Olympics in history: ~$51 billion including regional infrastructure, against a $12B budget. Opposition politician Boris Nemtsov, a Sochi native, published reports estimating that $25-30B was embezzled; he was shot four times on a bridge near the Kremlin on Feb 27, 2015. Russia&#x27;s state doping programme was exposed by Grigory Rodchenkov, former Moscow anti-doping lab director, now under U.S. witness protection.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2014-02-07</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.7342,43.6028,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Beijing - 2008/2022 Olympics / Dual Host</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Beijing is the only city to host both Summer (2008) and Winter (2022) Olympics. Estimates put the 2008 Games at $40-44B (official figure about $15B) - the most expensive Summer Olympics. Rights groups documented evictions, surveillance expansion and press restrictions. The 2022 Games went ahead amid UN and satellite-imagery documentation of mass detention in Xinjiang.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2008-01-01</begin><end>2008-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>116.4074,39.9042,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Berlin - 1936 Olympics / Nazi Propaganda</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Olympiastadion. Hitler&#x27;s Olympics - the template for international sport as state propaganda. Leni Riefenstahl&#x27;s &#x27;Olympia&#x27; (1938) set the look of sports filmmaking. Jesse Owens won four gold medals and returned to a segregated America. Coca-Cola&#x27;s German subsidiary supplied and promoted the drink at the Games. The IOC kept the Games in Nazi Germany and the U.S. took part.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1936-01-01</begin><end>1936-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>13.2395,52.5147,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Munich - 1972 Olympics / Black September</name><styleUrl>#t-conflict</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; conflict</p><p>Olympiapark. Sept 5, 1972: Black September killed two members of the Israeli team and took nine hostage; the West German rescue attempt at Fuerstenfeldbruck airbase failed - all nine hostages, five attackers and one police officer died (11 Israelis in all: 6 coaches, 5 athletes). Israel followed with Mossad&#x27;s Operation Wrath of God. IOC President Avery Brundage, who had been involved with the 1936 Games, kept the Olympics going.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1972-09-05</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.5461,48.1735,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Atlanta - 1996 Olympics / Centennial Park Bombing</name><styleUrl>#t-conflict</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; conflict</p><p>Centennial Olympic Park bombing, July 27, 1996. Pipe bomb killed Alice Hawthorne, injured 111. Eric Robert Rudolph was the bomber — domestic terrorist who also bombed abortion clinics and a lesbian nightclub. Security guard Richard Jewell, who discovered the bomb and saved lives, was falsely accused by the FBI and destroyed by media coverage. The FBI&#x27;s incorrect investigation ruined an innocent man while the actual bomber evaded capture for 5 years. The Olympics as a target, the investigation as a pattern of institutional failure.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>1996-07-27</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-84.393,33.7604,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Tilman Fertitta - Toyota Center, Houston</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Owns the Houston Rockets (bought for $2.2B, 2017) and Landry&#x27;s (Golden Nugget casinos, restaurants). Reported lead shareholder in Wynn Resorts (2025). U.S. Ambassador to Italy since May 2025. An NBA team owner who also owns casinos, in a league whose integrity rules bar players from betting.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2017-01-01</begin><end>2017-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-95.3621,29.7508,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>MGM Resorts HQ - Las Vegas</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>MGM Resorts International (founded by Kirk Kerkorian). Official gaming partner of major U.S. leagues; BetMGM is a 50/50 joint venture with Entain (formerly GVC Holdings), formed 2018. Revenue $17.5B (2025). Its Strip portfolio came largely from the 2000 Mirage Resorts merger (Bellagio, Mirage - sold to Hard Rock in 2022) and the 2005 Mandalay merger.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2023-01-01</begin><end>2023-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-115.1684,36.1024,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>FanDuel HQ - New York</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Parent company: Flutter Entertainment (registered in Dublin, operational headquarters New York; NYSE-listed), the world&#x27;s largest online betting company - owner of FanDuel, Paddy Power, Betfair, PokerStars, Sky Betting &amp; Gaming and Sportsbet. FanDuel is an official partner of U.S. leagues.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-73.9897,40.741,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>DraftKings HQ - Boston</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Founded 2012 in Boston as a daily fantasy sports operator under the fantasy-sports carve-out in the Unlawful Internet Gambling Enforcement Act; moved into sportsbooks after Murphy v. NCAA (2018). Official partner of major U.S. leagues.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2012-01-01</begin><end>2012-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-71.0405,42.3482,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Kroenke Sports &amp; Entertainment - Denver</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Ball Arena, Denver. Stan Kroenke (married to Walmart heiress Ann Walton Kroenke) owns the LA Rams (NFL), Denver Nuggets (NBA), Colorado Avalanche (NHL), Colorado Rapids (MLS), Colorado Mammoth (NLL) and Arsenal FC; KSE agreed in Sept 2026 to buy the Los Angeles Angels (MLB), pending approval. One family&#x27;s franchises across sports and two countries.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-105.0077,39.7487,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Madison Square Garden - Dolan / MSG Networks</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Home of the NY Knicks (NBA) and NY Rangers (NHL), owned by Madison Square Garden Sports. James Dolan, son of Cablevision founder Charles Dolan and former Cablevision CEO, runs MSG Sports and MSG Entertainment (the venue) and chairs Sphere Entertainment and MSG Networks (regional sports broadcasting). The family&#x27;s companies take revenue at venue, broadcast, advertising and tickets.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-73.9934,40.7505,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Crypto.com Arena - AEG / Philip Anschutz</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Formerly Staples Center. Philip Anschutz&#x27;s AEG owns the LA Kings (NHL) and LA Galaxy (MLS) and operates venues worldwide. Anschutz also owns the Washington Examiner, owned The Weekly Standard (bought 2009, closed 2018) and the San Francisco Examiner (2004-2011); previously Regal Cinemas and Qwest; oil and gas. Sports, venues and conservative media under one private owner.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-118.2673,34.043,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Chase Center - San Francisco / Lacob-Guber</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Golden State Warriors arena. Principal owner Joe Lacob is a former partner at Kleiner Perkins; co-owner Peter Guber is a former chairman of Sony Pictures (Mandalay Entertainment). Venture capital and Hollywood money in NBA ownership.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>-122.3877,37.768,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Barclays Center - Brooklyn / Joe Tsai</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Brooklyn Nets arena. Joe Tsai - Alibaba co-founder and chairman (since 2023) - bought 49% of the Nets in 2017 (team valued at $2.3B) and the rest in 2019, buying the arena for nearly $1B separately. Also owns the New York Liberty (WNBA), San Diego Seals and Las Vegas Desert Dogs (NLL). China&#x27;s 2017 National Intelligence Law obliges companies there to assist state intelligence work - a fact about the jurisdiction, not about the owner.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2019-01-01</begin><end>2019-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.9754,40.6826,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>U.S. Supreme Court - Murphy v. NCAA (2018)</name><styleUrl>#t-governance</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; governance</p><p>May 14, 2018: the Supreme Court held that PASPA (1992) violated the anti-commandeering doctrine (7-2) and struck the whole law (6-3); Justice Alito wrote for the majority. State-by-state legalization followed rapidly.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2018-05-14</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0044,38.8907,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Calabasas Crash Site - N72EX / Kobe Bryant</name><styleUrl>#t-conflict</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; conflict</p><p>Jan 26, 2020. Sikorsky S-76B (N72EX, built 1991), 9 killed including Kobe Bryant. Formerly the State of Illinois&#x27; N761LL (2007-2015). Island Express removed the cockpit voice recorder shortly after acquiring it (March 2016); no flight data recorder and no terrain warning system (not required). The operator&#x27;s certificate was VFR-only. Pilot Ara Zobayan: 8,577 hours, ~75 IFR. NTSB probable cause (Feb 2021): the pilot&#x27;s decision to continue VFR flight into instrument conditions, leading to spatial disorientation and loss of control; self-induced pressure contributed.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2020-01-26</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.6917,34.1369,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Island Express Helicopters - Long Beach, CA</name><styleUrl>#t-corporate</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; corporate</p><p>Operator of N72EX. Removed the cockpit voice recorder after acquiring the helicopter (March 2016). Its Part 135 certificate allowed VFR-only flights. After the crash it called it &#x27;an act of God&#x27; and cross-claimed against FAA controllers. The NTSB nonetheless determined probable cause: pilot continued VFR flight into cloud.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2016-03-01</begin><end>2016-03-28</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-118.1513,33.8178,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Sikorsky / Lockheed Martin - Stratford, CT</name><styleUrl>#t-military</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; military</p><p>Manufacturer of the S-76 and UH-60 Black Hawk; owned by United Aircraft/United Technologies 1929-2015 and by Lockheed Martin since Nov 2015. Its MATRIX autonomy technology (from DARPA&#x27;s ALIAS program) flies the optionally piloted Black Hawk. The 1991 S-76B N72EX had no such system; the NTSB found pilot disorientation in cloud.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>1991-01-01</begin><end>1991-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-73.1332,41.1865,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Colosseum - Rome (The Original &#x27;Baal Game&#x27;)</name><styleUrl>#t-archaeological</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; archaeological</p><p>Opened 80 AD under Emperor Titus. Capacity 50,000-80,000. Gladiatorial combat, animal hunts, executions, naval battles. Free admission — funded by the state (et circenses). The operational template: spectacle as social control. The population is fed and entertained; the rulers govern without resistance. The Colosseum was not entertainment — it was governance infrastructure. Bread and circuses is not a metaphor. It is an engineering specification. Every modern stadium is an iteration of this design: public subsidy, private profit, mass distraction, political utility.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>12.4922,41.8902,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Circus Maximus - Rome</name><styleUrl>#t-archaeological</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; archaeological</p><p>Chariot-racing venue; probably ~150,000 seats (Pliny&#x27;s 250,000 is considered too high). Used from Rome&#x27;s regal era until the last recorded races in 549 AD. Racing factions (Blues, Greens, Reds, Whites) worked like franchises with patrons and betting. The same factions drove the Nika riots of 532 AD at the Hippodrome of Constantinople, which nearly toppled Justinian - about 30,000 killed.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>12.4853,41.8862,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Olympia, Greece - Ancient Olympic Games</name><styleUrl>#t-archaeological</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; archaeological</p><p>The Olympic Games were traditionally founded here in 776 BC as a religious festival to Zeus, with a sacred truce (ekecheiria) between city-states. Athletes competed nude; married women were excluded (per Pausanias). The last recorded games were in 393 AD under Theodosius I; they likely ended under Theodosius II. The IOC (founded 1894) revived the Games in 1896 without the religious content.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>21.63,37.6388,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>The Venetian Macao / Cotai Strip, Macau</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>The anchor of Macau&#x27;s Cotai Strip, the gambling capital of the world, whose revenues long dwarfed Las Vegas; the VIP junket system that fed it collapsed when Suncity chief Alvin Chau — whose firm held ~40% of the junket market and handled bets prosecutors valued at HK$824 billion — was jailed 18 years in 2023 for running an illegal gambling and criminal syndicate. Source: TIME; South China Morning Post; Al Jazeera.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2023-01-01</begin><end>2023-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>113.5605,22.1482,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Crown Melbourne</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>A 2021 Victorian royal commission led by former judge Ray Finkelstein found Crown Melbourne had facilitated money laundering, had ties to organised-crime-linked junkets and serious governance failures, and was unsuitable to hold Victoria&#x27;s only casino licence. Regulators later fined it A$80M (2022, illicit China UnionPay transactions) and A$120M (responsible-gambling breaches). Source: Wikipedia; The Guardian Australia.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2021-01-01</begin><end>2021-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>144.9583,-37.8233,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>The Star Sydney</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Two NSW inquiries (2022 and the 2024 Bell review) found The Star unsuitable to hold a Sydney casino licence, exposing anti-money-laundering failures — including &#x27;Salon 95,&#x27; a secret VIP room with a hidden cash cage — and cash disguised as hotel expenses; the operator was fined AU$100 million and placed under a government manager. Source: NSW Independent Casino Commission (nicc.nsw.gov.au); iGaming Business; Yogonet.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2022-01-01</begin><end>2022-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>151.1956,-33.8687,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Wynn Las Vegas - $130M Money-Laundering Forfeiture</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>In September 2024 Wynn Las Vegas forfeited $130.1 million under a non-prosecution agreement, admitting it conspired with unlicensed money-transmitting businesses (&#x27;Flying Money&#x27; and &#x27;Human Head&#x27; gambling) to move funds for foreign patrons outside U.S. reporting rules - believed to be the largest forfeiture by a casino based on admissions of criminal wrongdoing. Source: DOJ / HSI (6 Sept 2024).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2024-09-01</begin><end>2024-09-28</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-115.1658,36.1264,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>bet365 Headquarters, Stoke-on-Trent</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Founded in 2000 by Denise Coates from a portable building in a Stoke-on-Trent car park, bet365 grew into one of the world&#x27;s largest online gambling firms, serving over 90 million customers; Coates has drawn scrutiny for pay packages exceeding £250 million in a single year. Source: CNN; BBC; Wikipedia, &#x27;bet365&#x27;.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2000-01-01</begin><end>2000-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-2.1995,53.0248,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Flutter Entertainment (registered seat), Dublin</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Flutter is the world&#x27;s largest online betting company, owner of FanDuel, PokerStars, Paddy Power, Betfair and Sky Bet; formed by the 2020 merger of Paddy Power Betfair and The Stars Group, it dominates the U.S. sports-betting market via FanDuel while keeping its registered base in Dublin. Source: Flutter Entertainment (flutter.com); Wikipedia; CNN Business.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2020-01-01</begin><end>2020-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-6.241,53.2938,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Hong Kong Jockey Club - Happy Valley Racecourse</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>The Jockey Club holds a legal monopoly on Hong Kong betting and is the city&#x27;s single largest taxpayer, contributing a record HK$35.9 billion to the community in 2022/23; its 2025/26 racing turnover reached HK$143.3 billion, and its charities trust ranks among the world&#x27;s largest donors. Source: South China Morning Post; Hong Kong Jockey Club (corporate.hkjc.com).</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.187,22.2672,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Malta Gaming Authority, Kalkara</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Malta licenses much of Europe&#x27;s online betting. The Daphne Project (OCCRP/IRPI, 2018) documented Italian mafia use of Maltese-licensed sites: a Palermo prosecutor called online gambling an &#x27;ATM with a continuous cash flow&#x27; for the clans, and one Cosa Nostra-linked operator, with &#x27;Ndrangheta brokers, grew profits to EUR 16 million a month on Maltese licences. In 2015 the MGA suspended Betuniq and Betsolution4U after an Italian probe; six Italians including &#x27;Ndrangheta point-man Mario Gennaro were arrested. Source: OCCRP (Daphne Project); The Shift News.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2017-01-01</begin><end>2017-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>14.5275,35.8969,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Illegal Sports-Betting Ring - U.S. Courthouse, Santa Ana</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>At this federal courthouse in 2025, Orange County bookmaker Mathew Bowyer was sentenced to a year and a day for running an illegal sports-gambling business used by Shohei Ohtani&#x27;s interpreter Ippei Mizuhara, who placed at least 19,000 bets and stole nearly $17 million from the MLB star to cover his losses. Source: U.S. Department of Justice (justice.gov); ESPN; Sportico.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeStamp><when>2025-08-29</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-117.8711,33.7481,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark><Placemark><name>Entain plc - London office (HQ: Douglas, Isle of Man)</name><styleUrl>#t-sports-gambling</styleUrl><description><![CDATA[<p><b>Sport and gambling</b> &middot; sports-gambling</p><p>Entain - owner of Ladbrokes, Coral, bwin and partypoker and co-owner of BetMGM; headquartered in Douglas, Isle of Man. In Dec 2023 it entered the UK&#x27;s first Crown Prosecution Service deferred prosecution agreement, paying £615 million in total over GVC&#x27;s failure to prevent bribery (Bribery Act 2010, s.7) in its former Turkish-facing business, 2011-2017. Source: CPS; Wikipedia.</p><p><a href="https://www.b0b.dev/report#SPORTS">View in report</a></p>]]></description><TimeSpan><begin>2023-01-01</begin><end>2023-12-31</end></TimeSpan><Point><coordinates>-0.0835,51.5113,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark></Folder></Document></kml>
